id: pm-csv-02hc
title: pm shell-outs silently degrade to wrong answers when stdout exceeds the spawnSync buffer
description: "Defect class: index.ts shelled out to `pm` via spawnSync in ~11 places. Node caps\ncaptured stdout at maxBuffer; when a tracker's output exceeds it the child is\nkilled mid-capture and spawnSync returns status=null, EMPTY stderr, truncated\nstdout. Guards that branch on `status !== 0` then read `null !== 0` as truthy and\nroute the overrun onto their failure branch, turning a buffer overflow into a\nWRONG ANSWER instead of an error:\n\n- itemStatus (pm get) mapped a null status onto `undefined` (\"item does not\n  exist\"), so the compensation guard skipped closes it should have run.\n- loadAppliedByTransaction (pm list-all) mapped a null/error result onto an\n  EMPTY applied-rows map, so a resumed atomic import silently re-imported every\n  already-applied row.\n\nTwo-tier remedy applied at every spawnSync site:\n- Tier 1 (delete the subprocess for reads, read the store in-process with NO\n  stdout ceiling): NOT used. A static @unbrained/pm-cli value import regresses\n  this package's documented standalone-loadability (dist/ must load with only its\n  own files; confirmed ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND when loaded off the package\n  resolution path), the task forbids dynamic import(), and the host-injected\n  ctx.sdk.client.list carries its own read-output budget — so a truly\n  ceiling-free in-process read is not achievable here.\n- Tier 2 (cap stdout at pmListMaxBuffer 16 MiB and route every status===null\n  overrun through a shared assertPmOutputFit as a hard, named error): applied to\n  ALL pm subprocess sites. itemStatus additionally gains the maxBuffer it lacked\n  (it was the only read still on Node's 1 MiB default). Writes that previously\n  lacked a buffer now carry one too. The two best-effort compensation sites keep\n  their tolerant behavior (no assert) by design.\n\nCompanion epic: pm-cli-website-wz6y (pm-csv is the worst offender tracked there)."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 2
tags: []
created_at: "2026-08-08T22:59:14.349Z"
